.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
Use \fBprof860 \fR\fRto interpret a profile file produced by the \fB\
monitor(3C)\fR\fR\& function. The symbol table in the executable \fR\fI\
prog\fI\fR\& is read and correlated with the profile file \fR\fI\
mdata\fI\fR\&. For each external text symbol the percentage of time spent executing between the address of that symbol and the address of the \
next is displayed, together with the number of times that function was called and the average number \
of milliseconds per call.
.LP
A program creates a profile file if it has been linked with the \fB\
\&-p\fR\fR\& option of \fBicc\fR\fR\& or \fBif77\fR\fR\&. This option \
arranges for calls to \fBmonitor(3C)\fR\fR\& at the beginning and end of execution. It is the call to \fB\
monitor(3C)\fR\fR\& at the end of execution that causes a profile file to be written. The number of calls to a function is \
tallied if the \fB\&-p\fR\fR\& option was used when the file containing the function was compiled.
.LP
The name of the file created by a profiled program is controlled by the environment variable \fR\fI\
PROFDIR\fI\fR\&. If \fR\fIPROFDIR\fI\fR\& does not exist,\fR\fI\
\& nnn.mon.out\fI\fR\&, where \fR\fInnn\fI\fR\& is the node ID of the node in the \
allocated cube (left-padded with zeroes, if necessary to generate three digits), is produced in the \
directory that is current when the program terminates. If \fR\fI\
PROFDIR\fI\fR\& = \fR\fIstring\fI\fR\&, then \fR\fIstring/nnn.progname\fI\fR\
\& is produced, where \fR\fIprogname\fI\fR\& consists of \fR\fI\
argv[0]\fI\fR\& with any path prefix removed, and \fR\fInnn\fI\fR\
\& is the program's node. If \fR\fIPROFDIR\fI\fR\& is the null string, no profiling output is \
produced.
.LP
The times reported in successive identical runs may vary by 5% or more, because of varying \
cache-hit ratios due to sharing of the cache with other processes.  Even if a program seems to be the \
only one using the machine, hidden background or asynchronous processes may blur the data.  In \
rare cases, the clock ticks initiating recording of the program counter may beat with loops in a \
program, grossly distorting measurements.
.LP
Call counts are always recorded precisely.
.LP
Only programs that call \fBexit(2)\fR\fR\& or return from \fB\
main\fR\fR\& will cause a profile file to be produced, unless \
a final call to \fBmonitor(3C)\fR\fR\& is explicitly coded.
.LP
The use of the \fB\&-p\fR\fR\& option to invoke profiling imposes a limit of 1000 functions that may have call \
counters established during program execution. For more counters, you must call \fB\
monitor(3C) \fR\fRdirectly. The number of call counters used will be reported automatically by the \fB\
prof860\fR\fR\& command whenever the number exceeds 5/6 of the maximum.
.LP
Idle time spent waiting for communication or I/O is credited to the \fB\
flick()\fR\fR\& system call.
.LP
\fBprof860 \fR\fRis not for use with programs loaded with remote host software.
.SH SEE ALSO
